1
00:00:02,072 --> 00:00:13,495
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I just got a couple of passages from a sutta in the Majjhima Nikaya, Majjhima 74, Dighanaka.

2
00:00:17,176 --> 00:00:23,817
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the one where the wanderer Dighanaka says,

3
00:00:27,367 --> 00:00:33,850
[SPEAKER_00]: I hold the view that everything is unacceptable to me.

4
00:00:37,711 --> 00:00:46,054
[SPEAKER_00]: But, later on in the sutta, the Buddha starts talking about the body and the feeling.

5
00:00:47,955 --> 00:00:54,638
[SPEAKER_00]: He says, but, āgīvēsana, this body, which is material,

6
00:00:56,375 --> 00:01:24,480
[SPEAKER_00]: Made of the four great elements, produced by mother and father, an accumulation of rice and porridge, of the nature of impermanence, rubbing, crushing, disintegration and destruction, should be seen as impermanent, as an arrow, as misery, as an affliction, as other, as disintegrating, as empty, as not self.

7
00:01:28,924 --> 00:01:31,546
[SPEAKER_00]: What do all those things have in common?

8
00:01:31,606 --> 00:01:32,366
[SPEAKER_01]: All those epithets.

9
00:01:36,248 --> 00:01:37,589
[SPEAKER_00]: What do they have in common?

10
00:01:38,229 --> 00:01:39,910
[SPEAKER_00]: What's a common thread through all of them?

11
00:01:43,492 --> 00:01:44,093
[SPEAKER_00]: Read over again.

12
00:01:44,313 --> 00:01:44,553
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

13
00:01:44,853 --> 00:01:52,657
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, so the beginning said that it's material, it's made of the four great elements, it's made by mother and father,

14
00:01:54,702 --> 00:01:55,823
[SPEAKER_00]: It's accumulated.

15
00:01:55,983 --> 00:01:58,626
[SPEAKER_00]: It's an accumulation of rice and porridge.

16
00:01:59,186 --> 00:02:02,409
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the nature of impermanence, robbing, crushing, disintegration, destruction.

17
00:02:02,429 --> 00:02:10,537
[SPEAKER_00]: Robbing, crushing, disintegration, destruction, non-self, what all those things are there.

18
00:02:11,097 --> 00:02:12,319
[SPEAKER_00]: What all those things have in common.

19
00:02:12,979 --> 00:02:16,780
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, there, it's like, that it's basically, it's not mine.

20
00:02:16,820 --> 00:02:18,361
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not produced by me.

21
00:02:18,581 --> 00:02:20,902
[SPEAKER_00]: It's made by my mom and dad.

22
00:02:20,922 --> 00:02:28,965
[SPEAKER_01]: What's the main characteristic of something that would define... something is yours and something is not yours?

23
00:02:29,145 --> 00:02:30,505
[SPEAKER_00]: What's the difference between the two?

24
00:02:30,845 --> 00:02:37,247
[SPEAKER_00]: So, something that's mine is, in some way, there's some pleasure involved in it.

25
00:02:37,788 --> 00:02:38,808
[SPEAKER_00]: More...

26
00:02:41,146 --> 00:02:44,148
[SPEAKER_01]: It is because of the pleasure, sure, but how does that manifest?

27
00:02:44,989 --> 00:02:48,772
[SPEAKER_01]: Something that's yours, which, of course, it's yours because it's pleasant.

28
00:02:48,832 --> 00:02:49,112
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

29
00:02:49,532 --> 00:02:52,775
[SPEAKER_01]: How is that manifested?

30
00:02:54,056 --> 00:02:55,597
[SPEAKER_00]: How is the pleasure manifested?

31
00:02:55,617 --> 00:02:56,798
[SPEAKER_00]: No, how is that which is yours?

32
00:02:56,978 --> 00:02:59,060
[SPEAKER_00]: How is that which is mine manifested?

33
00:03:00,201 --> 00:03:01,762
[SPEAKER_01]: As opposed to that which is not yours.

34
00:03:03,183 --> 00:03:06,966
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you might experience pleasure on both accounts, but what's the difference?

35
00:03:06,986 --> 00:03:08,407
[SPEAKER_01]: How about it comes first?

36
00:03:10,705 --> 00:03:13,727
[SPEAKER_01]: No, both come first in a way, because both come from the same place.

37
00:03:14,147 --> 00:03:21,152
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, the one that is yours and the one that isn't yours are differentiated by the control or lack of it.

38
00:03:21,452 --> 00:03:21,793
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

39
00:03:22,993 --> 00:03:25,775
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly the common thread.

40
00:03:25,815 --> 00:03:26,736
[SPEAKER_01]: You have no control.

41
00:03:27,657 --> 00:03:27,877
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

42
00:03:28,277 --> 00:03:31,680
[SPEAKER_01]: And any of those descriptions, it just reinforces that same thing.

43
00:03:31,800 --> 00:03:32,020
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

44
00:03:32,660 --> 00:03:35,903
[SPEAKER_01]: You are not the master of that body, which is you.

45
00:03:36,163 --> 00:03:36,383
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

46
00:03:38,780 --> 00:03:41,141
[SPEAKER_01]: It's matter, so it's like... it's kind of opaque.

47
00:03:41,161 --> 00:03:42,241
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, it's matter, exactly.

48
00:03:42,261 --> 00:03:43,381
[SPEAKER_01]: It's four great elements.

49
00:03:43,801 --> 00:03:44,661
[SPEAKER_01]: You have no control over it.

50
00:03:44,681 --> 00:03:44,841
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

51
00:03:44,861 --> 00:03:49,623
[SPEAKER_01]: It's made by mother and father, so the way it came to be has nothing to do with you.

52
00:03:49,903 --> 00:03:52,323
[SPEAKER_01]: Accumulation of random rice and porridge.

53
00:03:52,363 --> 00:03:53,523
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, nothing to do with you.

54
00:03:53,543 --> 00:03:58,865
[SPEAKER_01]: Subject to rubbing, disintegration, friction, basically falling apart.

55
00:03:58,905 --> 00:04:00,285
[SPEAKER_01]: And it says, it's other.

56
00:04:00,745 --> 00:04:07,787
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's your body is closer to the... like, when you perceive others, you clearly imply their bodies are not yours.

57
00:04:07,807 --> 00:04:08,427
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

58
00:04:09,047 --> 00:04:09,687
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the nature...

59
00:04:09,707 --> 00:04:11,868
[SPEAKER_01]: I have no control over others to that extent.

60
00:04:12,168 --> 00:04:18,030
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, your body is closer to that, that you think others are, than it is what you think your body is for you.

61
00:04:18,190 --> 00:04:20,051
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's my... that's my translation.

62
00:04:20,331 --> 00:04:22,772
[SPEAKER_00]: So, para is...

63
00:04:22,792 --> 00:04:26,993
[SPEAKER_00]: I think the common translation for that is foreign or alien or something.

64
00:04:27,233 --> 00:04:28,714
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, just it's other.

65
00:04:28,774 --> 00:04:29,294
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like...

66
00:04:29,314 --> 00:04:29,574
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

67
00:04:30,154 --> 00:04:34,476
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you translate it as foreign, that's like other bodies are foreign to you, because it's not your body.

68
00:04:34,916 --> 00:04:38,337
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you want to regard your body as foreign as other bodies to you.

69
00:04:40,418 --> 00:04:41,318
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the thing.

70
00:04:41,378 --> 00:04:44,040
[SPEAKER_00]: So, at the beginning, he says the body is material.

71
00:04:44,060 --> 00:04:45,180
[SPEAKER_00]: It is made of the four great elements.

72
00:04:45,220 --> 00:04:46,361
[SPEAKER_00]: It's made by your mother and father.

73
00:04:46,701 --> 00:04:49,262
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, it says it should be seen as... Yeah.

74
00:04:49,282 --> 00:04:53,624
[SPEAKER_00]: ...should be seen as impermanent, suffering, as a disease, as an abscess, as an arrow.

75
00:04:54,244 --> 00:04:57,526
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if the body were not truly... Yeah.

76
00:04:57,546 --> 00:05:01,207
[SPEAKER_01]: ...impermanent, disease, an arrow and so on, you can't see it like that.

77
00:05:01,287 --> 00:05:01,407
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

78
00:05:01,627 --> 00:05:03,428
[SPEAKER_01]: But it is truly like that.

79
00:05:03,848 --> 00:05:06,590
[SPEAKER_01]: But the reason you don't see that, because you're ignoring it.

80
00:05:06,950 --> 00:05:07,330
[SPEAKER_01]: Literally.

81
00:05:07,390 --> 00:05:09,451
[SPEAKER_01]: You're ignoring those characteristics because they are

82
00:05:11,337 --> 00:05:14,560
[SPEAKER_01]: And, as the Buddha said, yeah, that ignorance is gratuitous.

83
00:05:14,720 --> 00:05:16,921
[SPEAKER_01]: It shouldn't be there, but it's beginningless.

84
00:05:17,462 --> 00:05:20,584
[SPEAKER_01]: There was no point before which you were not ignorant, then you suddenly became ignorant.

85
00:05:21,205 --> 00:05:26,469
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why you should make the effort to see it correctly, the way it actually is, and stop ignoring it.

86
00:05:26,689 --> 00:05:29,011
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will undo the ignorance or avidya.

87
00:05:29,691 --> 00:05:33,154
[SPEAKER_01]: So, can you say, then, that...

88
00:05:33,334 --> 00:05:35,616
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you should...

89
00:05:35,636 --> 00:05:39,159
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the body should be seen in this way, in these ways.

90
00:05:40,311 --> 00:05:44,332
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like these should be the significance of the body for you.

91
00:05:44,372 --> 00:05:44,532
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

92
00:05:44,892 --> 00:05:47,212
[SPEAKER_01]: The context of your daily experience should be that.

93
00:05:47,432 --> 00:05:48,033
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

94
00:05:48,053 --> 00:05:50,533
[SPEAKER_01]: Make the effort and establish it at the back of your mind.

95
00:05:51,093 --> 00:05:51,933
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what you're thinking.

96
00:05:51,973 --> 00:05:52,153
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

97
00:05:52,413 --> 00:05:56,354
[SPEAKER_01]: Let it endure then while you go about and do things with your body.

98
00:05:56,394 --> 00:06:09,017
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you will realize how less and less you'll be able to engage with things that require passion, that require taking body to be yours, that require you to ignore the nature of the body being subjected to destruction and non-control.

99
00:06:09,297 --> 00:06:09,497
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

100
00:06:11,675 --> 00:06:17,716
[SPEAKER_00]: Because I think people will begin by having this notion of, well, my body, and then I have to think of my body like that.

101
00:06:17,996 --> 00:06:19,637
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and so... and that's... that's what I'm saying.

102
00:06:19,657 --> 00:06:26,598
[SPEAKER_01]: The difference is between establishing the context and... and... and attending to it like that.

103
00:06:26,798 --> 00:06:29,939
[SPEAKER_01]: It's basically you... you can't do it, even if you want to.

104
00:06:29,959 --> 00:06:31,639
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not supposed to make your body.

105
00:06:31,779 --> 00:06:31,959
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

106
00:06:32,919 --> 00:06:34,660
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's... it's actually ugly.

107
00:06:34,920 --> 00:06:36,540
[SPEAKER_01]: Could you be doing it on the basis of

108
00:06:37,551 --> 00:06:38,731
[SPEAKER_01]: Your ignorance first.

109
00:06:38,751 --> 00:06:39,532
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, exactly.

110
00:06:39,572 --> 00:06:46,234
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'm ignoring the true nature of the body and now, on top of that, I'm just trying to paste the new nature that I've been told it's a better one.

111
00:06:46,574 --> 00:06:50,036
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how people usually misunderstand the whole satipaṭṭhāna for that matter.

112
00:06:50,116 --> 00:06:50,256
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

113
00:06:50,496 --> 00:06:57,999
[SPEAKER_01]: You think you should be attending to every second of your body and not let it slip, because it slips, it slips back into permanence, non-destruction and control.

114
00:06:58,830 --> 00:06:58,950
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

115
00:06:59,170 --> 00:07:00,331
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to surmount.

116
00:07:00,932 --> 00:07:02,293
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, there is the existence...

117
00:07:02,313 --> 00:07:04,194
[SPEAKER_01]: I have a control over this body to some extent.

118
00:07:04,694 --> 00:07:05,495
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not denying it.

119
00:07:05,815 --> 00:07:09,578
[SPEAKER_01]: But you want to see that it doesn't matter how much control you can exercise upon your body.

120
00:07:10,018 --> 00:07:15,262
[SPEAKER_01]: Fundamentally, it's fully within the fact that the body is given there first and you have no control over that.

121
00:07:15,842 --> 00:07:18,904
[SPEAKER_01]: And if the body were to be taken away, it will be taken away.

122
00:07:18,944 --> 00:07:19,805
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't stop that.

123
00:07:20,512 --> 00:07:32,225
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's... instead of trying to, like, override your sense of control, deny it, like, it's impermanent, it's ugly, it's impermanent, it's impermanent, you actually want to first acknowledge it, and then see beyond it, see the context that it's created to it.

124
00:07:33,186 --> 00:07:34,928
[SPEAKER_01]: And... and that's why it's effortless.

125
00:07:34,968 --> 00:07:35,889
[SPEAKER_01]: Mindfulness is done, right?

126
00:07:35,949 --> 00:07:36,129
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

127
00:07:36,690 --> 00:07:41,235
[SPEAKER_01]: If you establish the context that this body is subject to... to impermanence, to destruction, to everything else,

128
00:07:41,673 --> 00:07:42,574
[SPEAKER_01]: And you let it...

129
00:07:42,934 --> 00:07:47,038
[SPEAKER_01]: I am subjected to that because that's what the nature of the body is.

130
00:07:47,058 --> 00:07:47,318
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

131
00:07:47,718 --> 00:07:49,380
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, I just need to protect that context.

132
00:07:49,540 --> 00:07:50,500
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't get carried away.

133
00:07:50,561 --> 00:07:51,481
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't forget about it.

134
00:07:51,581 --> 00:07:53,143
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't get distracted in regard to it.

135
00:07:53,863 --> 00:08:00,709
[SPEAKER_00]: So, but in order to do that, you need to sort of... it's almost like rewrite the significance of body for you.

136
00:08:01,330 --> 00:08:03,792
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you have these notions of body as

137
00:08:04,899 --> 00:08:05,139
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

138
00:08:05,340 --> 00:08:05,680
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

139
00:08:05,720 --> 00:08:08,084
[SPEAKER_00]: My body, you know, that I need to worry about.

140
00:08:08,104 --> 00:08:08,745
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you need to.

141
00:08:08,805 --> 00:08:11,408
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to see a problem with the way things are for you.

142
00:08:11,669 --> 00:08:11,829
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

143
00:08:12,209 --> 00:08:15,234
[SPEAKER_01]: In order to start making effort to maybe fix it.

144
00:08:15,434 --> 00:08:15,614
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

145
00:08:15,975 --> 00:08:16,496
[SPEAKER_01]: Sort it out.

146
00:08:16,736 --> 00:08:17,777
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

147
00:08:17,797 --> 00:08:20,141
[SPEAKER_01]: But what you need to do first, truly, is restrain.

148
00:08:21,350 --> 00:08:35,070
[SPEAKER_01]: The actions that are fully rooted in you taking body to be yours, that you are in control of it, that you have every right to it, that you're fully entitled to it, that you... like, there is nothing wrong with getting pleasure through the body.

149
00:08:35,831 --> 00:08:38,893
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, you know, the body is yours, the pleasure is available, why not?

150
00:08:39,133 --> 00:08:39,654
[SPEAKER_01]: All these things.

151
00:08:39,834 --> 00:08:44,116
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to start restraining that, in order to get to see it correctly.

152
00:08:44,196 --> 00:08:50,601
[SPEAKER_01]: And you wouldn't do that unless you see... unless you have understood, to some extent, that there is a problem with the way things are.

153
00:08:50,721 --> 00:08:51,121
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

154
00:08:52,622 --> 00:08:56,424
[SPEAKER_01]: Problem... main problem being you're subjected to suffering on account of that very body.

155
00:08:56,825 --> 00:08:57,065
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

156
00:08:57,565 --> 00:09:01,688
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because if you aren't changing your behavior, you are simply contradicting

157
00:09:02,858 --> 00:09:05,880
[SPEAKER_01]: The direction of... Oh, yeah, yeah, of course, of course.

158
00:09:06,240 --> 00:09:12,283
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's like, like as we said before, you know, your goal is to, to, to... and the Sutta says the same, actually.

159
00:09:12,303 --> 00:09:13,384
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a simile from the Sutta.

160
00:09:13,404 --> 00:09:26,331
[SPEAKER_01]: Your goal is to light the fire by collecting some, some, like, good wood that you can basically rub and create a spark and get the whole fire going.

161
00:09:27,752 --> 00:09:29,573
[SPEAKER_01]: But you keep that wood in the water.

162
00:09:30,594 --> 00:09:30,774
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

163
00:09:31,394 --> 00:09:33,736
[SPEAKER_01]: And you're not making effort to take it out and dry it out.

164
00:09:34,296 --> 00:09:37,499
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you're trying to make the fire at the same time with the wet wood.

165
00:09:38,219 --> 00:09:40,220
[SPEAKER_01]: It's utterly, completely futile.

166
00:09:41,521 --> 00:09:46,265
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly people who are practicing dhamma and the teaching without keeping the precepts.

167
00:09:47,186 --> 00:09:48,967
[SPEAKER_01]: Or even without being celibate.

168
00:09:49,447 --> 00:09:49,908
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't.

169
00:09:53,850 --> 00:09:55,211
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's just contradiction in terms.

170
00:10:00,657 --> 00:10:05,721
[SPEAKER_00]: So, so, the Buddha's... goes on.

171
00:10:05,761 --> 00:10:17,109
[SPEAKER_00]: So, so, one should see the body as impermanent, as suffering, as a disease, as an abscess, as an arrow, as misery, as an affliction, as other, as disintegrating, as empty, as not-self.

172
00:10:18,030 --> 00:10:29,939
[SPEAKER_00]: For one, seeing this body as impermanent, as suffering, like this, eh... whatever desire for the body, affection for the body, giving in to the body, that is a bandha.

173
00:10:32,097 --> 00:10:44,661
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you maintain the context of the correct nature of the body, all the things that have been dependent upon absence of that context will disappear.

174
00:10:44,961 --> 00:10:48,362
[SPEAKER_01]: Things such as desire, ill-will, wrong views.

175
00:10:48,882 --> 00:10:49,122
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

176
00:10:51,865 --> 00:10:52,746
[SPEAKER_01]: But see, that's the thing.

177
00:10:53,146 --> 00:10:55,208
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to see the body correctly for what it is.

178
00:10:55,788 --> 00:11:06,617
[SPEAKER_01]: Because now, when your mind is affected with ignorance, when the only way you relate your body is through sensuality, what you think the body is, it's basically the product of you taking wrongly your own body.

179
00:11:06,737 --> 00:11:07,938
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not what the body is.

180
00:11:08,979 --> 00:11:15,645
[SPEAKER_01]: So you need to start restraining that and then start undoing it in order to arrive at the point where you recognize this is what my body is.

181
00:11:16,867 --> 00:11:18,768
[SPEAKER_01]: And then dwell and develop that context.

182
00:11:18,808 --> 00:11:19,048
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

183
00:11:19,068 --> 00:11:19,608
[SPEAKER_00]: Recollection.

184
00:11:19,648 --> 00:11:24,030
[SPEAKER_00]: Recollection is simply recollectedness of the body, mindfulness of the body.

185
00:11:24,470 --> 00:11:27,311
[SPEAKER_00]: It's basically just understanding what the body is.

186
00:11:27,331 --> 00:11:27,672
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

187
00:11:28,672 --> 00:11:30,473
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to fabricate anything new there.

188
00:11:30,893 --> 00:11:34,254
[SPEAKER_01]: You just need to undo stuff that's obscuring it.

189
00:11:34,474 --> 00:11:35,635
[SPEAKER_01]: From which you're responsible.

190
00:11:35,855 --> 00:11:36,535
[SPEAKER_00]: Covering up.

191
00:11:36,875 --> 00:11:37,056
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

192
00:11:37,356 --> 00:11:38,056
[SPEAKER_01]: Actively covering up.

193
00:11:38,076 --> 00:11:39,717
[SPEAKER_00]: The right understanding of what body is.

194
00:11:39,777 --> 00:11:39,997
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

195
00:11:42,220 --> 00:11:50,926
[SPEAKER_01]: The obvious nature, that will become obvious and apparent here and now, if you stop looking away from it, if you stop ignoring it.

196
00:11:55,109 --> 00:12:05,656
[SPEAKER_00]: There's another sutta in the Sanghuta Nikaya, the Khandha Sanghuta, that says that an instructed bhikkhu

197
00:12:09,753 --> 00:12:20,796
[SPEAKER_00]: A virtuous bhikkhu should contemplate the five aggregates, matter, feeling, perception, determinations, consciousness, as these things.

198
00:12:22,216 --> 00:12:26,237
[SPEAKER_00]: And then he says, if he does, then he may become a sotāpanna.

199
00:12:26,337 --> 00:12:29,677
[SPEAKER_00]: And a sotāpanna should contemplate these things, if he does.

200
00:12:33,133 --> 00:12:39,615
[SPEAKER_00]: Body, matter, the five aggregates, all essentially have this same significance.

201
00:12:40,936 --> 00:12:41,356
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yes.

202
00:12:41,536 --> 00:12:46,138
[SPEAKER_01]: And not just that they have the same significance, same concept, same nature.

203
00:12:46,218 --> 00:12:47,318
[SPEAKER_01]: They're all within the same thing.

204
00:12:48,619 --> 00:12:54,641
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not that you start first with mindfulness of the body and then you go and find your feelings, your perceptions, your intentions.

205
00:12:55,861 --> 00:12:57,242
[SPEAKER_01]: Left and right from it.

206
00:12:57,282 --> 00:12:57,803
[SPEAKER_01]: It's within it.

207
00:12:58,063 --> 00:12:58,263
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

208
00:12:58,923 --> 00:13:02,626
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you have mindfulness of the body, right, you have mindfulness of the five aggregates, right?

209
00:13:03,247 --> 00:13:11,613
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can't experience, oh, this is my aggregate of form and it's separate from these other aggregates and you can kind of look between them and so on.

210
00:13:11,673 --> 00:13:12,874
[SPEAKER_01]: You are the whole five.

211
00:13:12,994 --> 00:13:14,095
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always the five.

212
00:13:14,635 --> 00:13:15,556
[SPEAKER_01]: Not three and a half.

213
00:13:17,057 --> 00:13:23,562
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whether you're mindful of the five aggregates or mindful of the body as the dirt, as the disease, as misery, you're mindful of the same thing.

214
00:13:23,722 --> 00:13:23,882
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

215
00:13:24,998 --> 00:13:26,799
[SPEAKER_01]: And this passion will be the result of it.

216
00:13:27,899 --> 00:13:31,261
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why even, like, he says, like, you can become Sotāpanna by doing it.

217
00:13:31,981 --> 00:13:32,902
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

218
00:13:33,022 --> 00:13:53,732
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, even as a newcomer, so to speak, a beginner to the practice, if you are actually, instead of trying to find a magical meditation technique that will sort your issues out, if you actually start practicing virtue, investing effort in sensory strength, and then dwell on the nature of what you already are,

219
00:13:54,963 --> 00:14:02,595
[SPEAKER_01]: Subject to death, subject to illness, subject to disease, to old age and all other accident, misfortune.

220
00:14:02,996 --> 00:14:05,419
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to wait and quite the opposite.

221
00:14:06,349 --> 00:14:07,950
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to wait for those things to happen.

222
00:14:08,370 --> 00:14:10,010
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to do it before those things happen.

223
00:14:10,771 --> 00:14:15,132
[SPEAKER_01]: But they are obviously there, which is the entire point of lay life for most people.

224
00:14:15,192 --> 00:14:19,554
[SPEAKER_01]: It's actually engaging with things that will help them distract themselves from the obvious.

225
00:14:20,274 --> 00:14:25,956
[SPEAKER_01]: That you will lose everything, that you will die, go sick, lose your health, lose your strength, lose your youth, lose all these things.

226
00:14:26,537 --> 00:14:30,218
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, all the effort you make is just to help you ignore that.

227
00:14:30,678 --> 00:14:32,239
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why people are afraid to be alone.

228
00:14:32,659 --> 00:14:34,240
[SPEAKER_01]: Afraid to be without distractions.

229
00:14:34,300 --> 00:14:38,584
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why boredom always turns into existential dread, if it's prolonged.

230
00:14:39,225 --> 00:14:46,010
[SPEAKER_01]: So, give me company, give me family, give me distraction, give me things to do, give me some purpose, because otherwise I will drown in myself.

231
00:14:46,070 --> 00:14:49,173
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and don't come to me about these things.

232
00:14:49,253 --> 00:14:53,637
[SPEAKER_01]: And often people then take on to practice the dhamma for those very wrong reasons.

233
00:14:53,817 --> 00:14:55,698
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because they want to undo what they are.

234
00:14:56,099 --> 00:15:00,883
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you just want dhamma then to take them on a whole new level of basically forgetting what they are.

235
00:15:02,985 --> 00:15:19,296
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you take the right order of practice, virtue, sense restraint, and then actually acknowledging the nature of your own existence, which is what mundane right view pretty much is, then getting the right view of a Sotāpanna, well, you won't have to wish it.

236
00:15:19,896 --> 00:15:23,679
[SPEAKER_01]: It will have to happen because you're not running away from your own existence anymore.

237
00:15:24,922 --> 00:15:31,787
[SPEAKER_00]: Mundane right view is... there is mother, there is father, there are... there is giving, there is suffering.

238
00:15:31,827 --> 00:15:32,708
[SPEAKER_00]: There is giving, yeah.

239
00:15:32,748 --> 00:15:38,072
[SPEAKER_00]: There are things that are unfathomable, there are things that are actions, there are results, there is observations.

240
00:15:38,713 --> 00:15:41,155
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically, there is responsibility.

241
00:15:41,175 --> 00:15:41,455
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

242
00:15:41,735 --> 00:15:41,935
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

243
00:15:42,216 --> 00:15:45,038
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, there is also, like, the fact that I don't know.

244
00:15:45,238 --> 00:15:46,439
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the lack of my knowledge.

245
00:15:46,519 --> 00:15:47,239
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the fact.

246
00:15:48,100 --> 00:15:49,221
[SPEAKER_01]: You admit all those things.

247
00:15:49,241 --> 00:15:49,942
[SPEAKER_01]: You acknowledge them.

248
00:15:50,879 --> 00:15:54,983
[SPEAKER_01]: So, mundane right view is basically the right acknowledgement or acknowledgement of the right things.

249
00:15:55,503 --> 00:15:58,726
[SPEAKER_01]: There are things that are not in my control, that my life depends upon.

250
00:15:59,587 --> 00:16:05,032
[SPEAKER_01]: And, again, you can ask anyone, even a non-Buddhist person who is not interested in meditation or any form of practice.

251
00:16:05,673 --> 00:16:08,976
[SPEAKER_01]: Fundamentally, they would come down and admit.

252
00:16:09,196 --> 00:16:12,819
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, of course, I know that I cannot escape death.

253
00:16:13,019 --> 00:16:13,240
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

254
00:16:13,500 --> 00:16:15,201
[SPEAKER_01]: I know that I cannot escape sickness.

255
00:16:16,462 --> 00:16:16,723
[SPEAKER_01]: But,

256
00:16:18,388 --> 00:16:21,269
[SPEAKER_01]: It take... it will take them a lot of effort just to admit that temporarily.

257
00:16:21,549 --> 00:16:24,790
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything else that they've done is to cover it up and forget about it.

258
00:16:27,451 --> 00:16:30,251
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly why you remain enslaved by those things.

259
00:16:30,591 --> 00:16:32,712
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of your own effort to remain.

260
00:16:33,072 --> 00:16:33,272
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

261
00:16:33,772 --> 00:16:34,493
[SPEAKER_01]: They're imprisoned.

262
00:16:36,173 --> 00:16:37,493
[SPEAKER_01]: Those things can't imprison you.

263
00:16:38,414 --> 00:16:40,174
[SPEAKER_00]: You imprison yourself through those things.

264
00:16:40,534 --> 00:16:44,975
[SPEAKER_00]: By... by... resp... everything you're doing is response to them.

265
00:16:45,316 --> 00:16:45,516
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

266
00:16:45,776 --> 00:16:46,196
[SPEAKER_01]: Absolutely.

267
00:16:46,456 --> 00:16:46,896
[SPEAKER_01]: Absolutely.

268
00:16:48,183 --> 00:16:50,624
[SPEAKER_01]: Possessions, ownership, everything, everything, everything.

269
00:16:52,804 --> 00:16:58,006
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's really what the right practice is.

270
00:16:59,246 --> 00:16:59,726
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing else.

271
00:16:59,846 --> 00:17:01,887
[SPEAKER_01]: No other technique necessary.

272
00:17:03,107 --> 00:17:07,508
[SPEAKER_01]: If you keep your sīla, restrain your senses and then admit what's obvious.

273
00:17:07,528 --> 00:17:08,068
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

274
00:17:09,148 --> 00:17:11,829
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't distract yourself from obvious context of your life.

275
00:17:11,949 --> 00:17:12,709
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the technique.

276
00:17:13,009 --> 00:17:13,169
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

277
00:17:13,450 --> 00:17:14,190
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your technique.

278
00:17:14,230 --> 00:17:14,870
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

279
00:17:16,446 --> 00:17:18,808
[SPEAKER_01]: But that can be very, very unpleasant.

280
00:17:22,690 --> 00:17:25,192
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's only unpleasant because you never dealt with it.

281
00:17:25,352 --> 00:17:26,552
[SPEAKER_01]: You never wanted to deal with it.

282
00:17:26,673 --> 00:17:28,274
[SPEAKER_01]: So now it's accumulated, lots of it.

283
00:17:28,774 --> 00:17:30,675
[SPEAKER_01]: Probably you didn't want to deal with it for lifetimes.

284
00:17:33,437 --> 00:17:34,758
[SPEAKER_00]: But the sooner you start, the better.

285
00:17:40,493 --> 00:17:42,374
[SPEAKER_00]: This sutta then goes on to talk about feelings.

286
00:17:43,355 --> 00:17:44,335
[SPEAKER_00]: It just says something.

287
00:17:44,355 --> 00:17:46,076
[SPEAKER_00]: I think it's quite interesting.

288
00:17:46,097 --> 00:17:49,519
[SPEAKER_00]: It says, Aggivāsana, there are these three feelings.

289
00:17:49,799 --> 00:17:53,321
[SPEAKER_00]: Pleasant feeling, painful feeling, neither painful nor pleasant feeling.

290
00:17:54,762 --> 00:17:55,162
[SPEAKER_00]: Aggivāsana...

291
00:17:55,182 --> 00:17:56,463
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know why he calls him Aggivāsana.

292
00:17:56,503 --> 00:17:57,684
[SPEAKER_00]: Anyway, Aggivāsana...

293
00:17:57,704 --> 00:17:58,784
[SPEAKER_00]: It was his clan's name.

294
00:17:58,824 --> 00:17:59,765
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what they say.

295
00:17:59,865 --> 00:18:00,385
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know.

296
00:18:00,405 --> 00:18:00,886
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not sure.

297
00:18:01,526 --> 00:18:05,989
[SPEAKER_00]: Anyway, Aggivāsana, on whatever occasion one feels a pleasant feeling,

298
00:18:07,162 --> 00:18:13,023
[SPEAKER_00]: On that occasion, one feels neither painful feeling, nor a neither painful nor pleasant feeling.

299
00:18:13,043 --> 00:18:15,364
[SPEAKER_00]: On that occasion, one feels only a pleasant feeling.

300
00:18:16,624 --> 00:18:22,865
[SPEAKER_00]: On whatever occasion one feels a painful feeling, one feels neither a pleasant feeling, nor a neither painful nor pleasant feeling.

301
00:18:23,105 --> 00:18:26,066
[SPEAKER_00]: On that occasion, one feels only a painful feeling.

302
00:18:28,066 --> 00:18:28,986
[SPEAKER_01]: And on whatever occasion...

303
00:18:29,026 --> 00:18:32,687
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, on whatever occasion what's felt, that's the only thing that's felt?

304
00:18:32,927 --> 00:18:33,187
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

305
00:18:34,243 --> 00:18:35,383
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the only thing that's felt.

306
00:18:35,744 --> 00:18:37,464
[SPEAKER_00]: There is only that feeling.

307
00:18:37,484 --> 00:18:46,868
[SPEAKER_00]: And I think, if you think about that, I think, generally, people wouldn't understand things like that.

308
00:18:46,908 --> 00:18:47,188
[SPEAKER_00]: No, of course.

309
00:18:47,208 --> 00:18:48,668
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they would say, well, no, hang on a minute.

310
00:18:48,688 --> 00:18:51,269
[SPEAKER_01]: It would take a lot of undoing to arrive just at that.

311
00:18:51,289 --> 00:18:51,449
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

312
00:18:52,430 --> 00:18:56,812
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you'll have a particular feeling right now,

313
00:18:59,858 --> 00:19:05,681
[SPEAKER_00]: Laced with desire, let's say, and it's for something, thinking that if I do that, then I'll have this other feeling.

314
00:19:05,781 --> 00:19:05,961
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

315
00:19:06,341 --> 00:19:08,102
[SPEAKER_00]: As if those two feelings are both present.

316
00:19:08,162 --> 00:19:08,302
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

317
00:19:08,342 --> 00:19:13,105
[SPEAKER_01]: Like that one in the future... Well, they are present to the assumed sense of self.

318
00:19:13,285 --> 00:19:13,505
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

319
00:19:13,645 --> 00:19:15,486
[SPEAKER_01]: That's independent of what I was being felt.

320
00:19:16,427 --> 00:19:22,530
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is a contradiction in terms, but it's a contradiction that will remain real as such for as long as you don't face it and undo it.

321
00:19:22,730 --> 00:19:23,050
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

322
00:19:24,092 --> 00:19:24,712
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the thing.

323
00:19:24,832 --> 00:19:27,214
[SPEAKER_01]: A common person thinks they're separate from their feelings.

324
00:19:27,254 --> 00:19:28,674
[SPEAKER_01]: Feelings happen to me.

325
00:19:28,894 --> 00:19:30,355
[SPEAKER_01]: I have this feeling, I have that feeling.

326
00:19:30,715 --> 00:19:33,477
[SPEAKER_01]: I remain unchanged throughout these feelings coming and going.

327
00:19:34,237 --> 00:19:40,320
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you look at it, you realize, the same as we described the body, you are subjected to the feeling, not other way around.

328
00:19:41,421 --> 00:19:44,462
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why the Buddha would ask people who would say, yes, feeling is mine.

329
00:19:44,602 --> 00:19:45,783
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, I am first.

330
00:19:45,923 --> 00:19:47,844
[SPEAKER_01]: Feelings belong to me as a result of it.

331
00:19:48,444 --> 00:19:50,485
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha would ask, well, which feeling then belongs to you?

332
00:19:50,625 --> 00:19:53,227
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you were in control of your own feelings,

333
00:19:54,011 --> 00:19:56,133
[SPEAKER_01]: You will never feel unpleasant.

334
00:19:56,213 --> 00:19:56,434
[SPEAKER_01]: Never.

335
00:19:57,395 --> 00:20:03,822
[SPEAKER_01]: Because literally, whenever this pleasure touches you, all you do, all your efforts are rooted in trying to overcome it.

336
00:20:05,324 --> 00:20:05,784
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything.

337
00:20:06,104 --> 00:20:11,070
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything is rooted in trying to overcome pain, yet here you are, claiming you are in charge of your pain.

338
00:20:12,165 --> 00:20:16,407
[SPEAKER_01]: But all those intentions to remove the pain are all within that pain.

339
00:20:16,507 --> 00:20:16,967
[SPEAKER_01]: Absolutely.

340
00:20:17,547 --> 00:20:20,028
[SPEAKER_01]: All intentions to protect the pleasure are within that pleasure.

341
00:20:20,448 --> 00:20:29,151
[SPEAKER_01]: All intentions to run away from the neither pain nor pleasure, as in boredom, basically, are within that feeling.

342
00:20:29,171 --> 00:20:32,392
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how can you... any attempt to escape the feeling...

343
00:20:33,393 --> 00:20:35,213
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible, because there's no outside of the feeling.

344
00:20:35,573 --> 00:20:36,674
[SPEAKER_01]: You're always within the feeling.

345
00:20:37,501 --> 00:20:40,942
[SPEAKER_01]: And the fact that they will change means you're not always within the same feeling.

346
00:20:41,142 --> 00:20:41,362
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

347
00:20:41,522 --> 00:20:45,464
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if it were just one feeling, well, you could then at least say, well, this feeling doesn't change.

348
00:20:45,544 --> 00:20:45,764
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

349
00:20:46,044 --> 00:20:46,704
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's okay.

350
00:20:47,205 --> 00:20:47,925
[SPEAKER_01]: But they do change.

351
00:20:47,985 --> 00:20:48,265
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

352
00:20:48,845 --> 00:20:56,828
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the container within which you're fully enclosed, that you cannot step outside of it, changes on its own, and that change is utterly external to you.

353
00:20:56,868 --> 00:20:58,549
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot... You can't even think about it.

354
00:20:58,789 --> 00:20:58,949
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

355
00:20:58,989 --> 00:21:01,049
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot even conceive it theoretically.

356
00:21:01,069 --> 00:21:01,490
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

357
00:21:01,610 --> 00:21:03,230
[SPEAKER_01]: How it becomes the other.

358
00:21:03,610 --> 00:21:04,951
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just subjected to this,

359
00:21:05,685 --> 00:21:06,126
[SPEAKER_01]: And then it endures.

360
00:21:06,146 --> 00:21:06,927
[SPEAKER_01]: And everything is covered by that.

361
00:21:06,947 --> 00:21:10,371
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you just find out you're subjected to something else, that that endures.

362
00:21:10,431 --> 00:21:11,693
[SPEAKER_00]: And everything is covered by that.

363
00:21:11,913 --> 00:21:13,095
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything is covered by that.

364
00:21:13,135 --> 00:21:14,216
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything is within that.

365
00:21:14,256 --> 00:21:21,826
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what's meant, like, the actual change, as in impermanence, as in subject to change, subject to impermanence, is abrupt.

366
00:21:22,106 --> 00:21:23,488
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not actually observable.

367
00:21:24,650 --> 00:21:27,671
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's observable, means you're separated from it, and you can observe it.

368
00:21:27,751 --> 00:21:29,212
[SPEAKER_01]: Point A and point B.

369
00:21:29,392 --> 00:21:29,532
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

370
00:21:29,912 --> 00:21:31,173
[SPEAKER_01]: You are within point A.

371
00:21:31,193 --> 00:21:32,714
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no point B within point A.

372
00:21:32,794 --> 00:21:33,034
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

373
00:21:33,714 --> 00:21:34,395
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just point A.

374
00:21:34,735 --> 00:21:36,856
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you realize, oh, now it's another point A.

375
00:21:37,556 --> 00:21:43,359
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can remember the previous one, but only on the on the ground of the current, present enduring feeling.

376
00:21:43,379 --> 00:21:50,122
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, you can have same pleasure experience in the future, but it's not the same pleasure in that sense, because it's another.

377
00:21:50,282 --> 00:21:50,502
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

378
00:21:51,731 --> 00:21:53,592
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, there is only one feeling.

379
00:21:53,752 --> 00:21:55,493
[SPEAKER_00]: There is only one feeling within which...

380
00:21:55,513 --> 00:21:58,014
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you can find other feelings, but they're all going to be within.

381
00:21:58,114 --> 00:21:59,555
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, yes, yeah, yeah.

382
00:21:59,575 --> 00:22:00,015
[SPEAKER_00]: This one...

383
00:22:00,035 --> 00:22:02,096
[SPEAKER_01]: Fundamentally, there is only one feeling felt.

384
00:22:02,376 --> 00:22:02,636
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

385
00:22:03,616 --> 00:22:05,537
[SPEAKER_01]: Pain, pleasure or neither.

386
00:22:06,117 --> 00:22:06,338
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

387
00:22:08,018 --> 00:22:18,503
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, realizing that, you realize that the whole subject to change, the whole the whole existence, it's actually not rooted in you and not rooted in your point of view.

388
00:22:19,099 --> 00:22:28,141
[SPEAKER_01]: But rooted in things arising on their own, as in I'm subjected to this feeling that has arisen on its own and so basically burdens me as such.

389
00:22:30,242 --> 00:22:40,304
[SPEAKER_01]: So, recognizing that that it's not rooted in you, basically, it's it's recognizing what truly impermanence is, as in subject to change.

390
00:22:41,064 --> 00:22:42,965
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is what the Buddha is saying.

391
00:22:43,045 --> 00:22:43,505
[SPEAKER_01]: Unownable.

392
00:22:44,212 --> 00:23:04,441
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he says, once he said there's only... one feels only one kind of feeling, he then says, āgibhāsana, pleasant feeling is impermanent, determined, dependently arisen, with the nature of... has the nature of wasting away, of passing away, of fading away, of cessation.

393
00:23:05,447 --> 00:23:06,508
[SPEAKER_00]: Same for painful feeling.

394
00:23:07,229 --> 00:23:09,851
[SPEAKER_01]: Same for neither painful nor pleasant feeling.

395
00:23:09,931 --> 00:23:14,375
[SPEAKER_01]: And the important thing for somebody who wants to contemplate this, is to realize there is no in-between.

396
00:23:15,836 --> 00:23:17,017
[SPEAKER_01]: You only find yourself.

397
00:23:17,377 --> 00:23:24,363
[SPEAKER_01]: You can only find yourself fully within already feeling that already endures.

398
00:23:24,383 --> 00:23:25,684
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

399
00:23:26,425 --> 00:23:29,107
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not there is pleasure-pleasure and now it's disappearing.

400
00:23:29,950 --> 00:23:30,870
[SPEAKER_01]: And now there is pain.

401
00:23:31,250 --> 00:23:35,512
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, that assumes you as an external point of view to this process.

402
00:23:36,692 --> 00:23:37,252
[SPEAKER_01]: But it isn't.

403
00:23:37,372 --> 00:23:39,453
[SPEAKER_01]: It can't be, because you are within it.

404
00:23:39,853 --> 00:23:42,214
[SPEAKER_01]: And that would also be putting you first.

405
00:23:42,494 --> 00:23:43,614
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, of course.

406
00:23:43,675 --> 00:23:44,895
[SPEAKER_01]: You're the one that's unchanged.

407
00:23:45,035 --> 00:23:46,095
[SPEAKER_01]: The process changes.

408
00:23:46,135 --> 00:23:46,756
[SPEAKER_01]: Feelings change.

409
00:23:46,816 --> 00:23:47,736
[SPEAKER_01]: I remain unchanged.

410
00:23:48,675 --> 00:23:56,180
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I might not be indifferent towards it, but you still have a grain of assumption that you are separate from it to the extent that you can observe two and in between how one becomes the other.

411
00:23:56,901 --> 00:23:57,041
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

412
00:23:57,321 --> 00:24:00,823
[SPEAKER_01]: You find yourself within feeling that already endures.

413
00:24:00,844 --> 00:24:01,004
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

414
00:24:01,664 --> 00:24:03,806
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, also, how does the feeling change?

415
00:24:04,026 --> 00:24:04,846
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not on you.

416
00:24:05,467 --> 00:24:05,687
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

417
00:24:06,957 --> 00:24:08,718
[SPEAKER_01]: See, how does the feeling change?

418
00:24:08,858 --> 00:24:12,841
[SPEAKER_01]: Question implies, how can I step outside of this feeling and see how it becomes another?

419
00:24:12,962 --> 00:24:15,043
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, how can I repossess it?

420
00:24:15,163 --> 00:24:19,186
[SPEAKER_01]: How can I regain the control of its own origin and cessation?

421
00:24:19,266 --> 00:24:20,427
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how I'll be safe.

422
00:24:20,467 --> 00:24:26,752
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why that view, it's not just like a level of theory, it's a deeply emotionally rooted view of...

423
00:24:26,792 --> 00:24:29,454
[SPEAKER_01]: I need safety in regard to this that I'm subjected to.

424
00:24:29,994 --> 00:24:33,637
[SPEAKER_01]: The safety is, if I repossess the way it came to be, I'll be in charge of it.

425
00:24:34,372 --> 00:24:38,435
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why you automatically start assuming the external from it, as a means of dealing with it.

426
00:24:40,056 --> 00:24:43,279
[SPEAKER_01]: But you realize, no, I have no say in it, and nor can I step outside of it.

427
00:24:43,359 --> 00:24:47,342
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, like, you have to stop acting out of your feelings, as in sense, restraint comes first.

428
00:24:47,822 --> 00:24:50,904
[SPEAKER_01]: Allow them to endure, and then you get to understand their nature.

429
00:24:52,185 --> 00:24:59,470
[SPEAKER_01]: You realize, doesn't matter, doesn't matter what feeling is present, whether it's pleasant, unpleasant, or neutral, your restraint has been developed beforehand.

430
00:25:00,031 --> 00:25:02,653
[SPEAKER_01]: And now you realize, and because it's present on its own,

431
00:25:03,445 --> 00:25:04,565
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it will have to change.

432
00:25:05,746 --> 00:25:07,126
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the understanding of sotāpanna.

433
00:25:07,186 --> 00:25:10,727
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever has arisen, that's why it's impermanent.

434
00:25:10,747 --> 00:25:12,367
[SPEAKER_01]: All of that has the nature to cease.

435
00:25:12,407 --> 00:25:13,607
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it has the nature to cease.

436
00:25:13,907 --> 00:25:18,488
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because I can observe its cessation, but because it came on its own and I'm not saying it.

437
00:25:18,928 --> 00:25:19,869
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that's its nature.

438
00:25:19,889 --> 00:25:20,749
[SPEAKER_01]: That's, yes, exactly.

439
00:25:20,769 --> 00:25:21,149
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not...

440
00:25:21,289 --> 00:25:22,629
[SPEAKER_01]: It's of its nature to cease.

441
00:25:22,669 --> 00:25:24,450
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like a, yeah, look, look, it disappeared.

442
00:25:24,470 --> 00:25:25,510
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's impermanent.

443
00:25:25,550 --> 00:25:25,750
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

444
00:25:26,470 --> 00:25:29,051
[SPEAKER_01]: That's almost like, yeah, you're... You're outside of it

445
00:25:32,932 --> 00:25:35,717
[SPEAKER_01]: So, recognizing there is no in-between the two feelings.

446
00:25:36,638 --> 00:25:38,962
[SPEAKER_01]: There is only one within which you are subjected to.

447
00:25:40,475 --> 00:25:41,436
[SPEAKER_01]: And you have to endure it.

448
00:25:42,016 --> 00:25:46,459
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you just... like, next time, you just... like, as we've done those reflections about what's your current mood.

449
00:25:46,539 --> 00:25:47,940
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it pleasant, unpleasant, neutral?

450
00:25:48,340 --> 00:25:49,041
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, it's pleasant.

451
00:25:49,361 --> 00:25:50,061
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now you're doing this.

452
00:25:50,121 --> 00:25:50,762
[SPEAKER_01]: You're thinking.

453
00:25:51,462 --> 00:25:52,303
[SPEAKER_01]: Time passes.

454
00:25:53,303 --> 00:25:54,464
[SPEAKER_01]: The same feeling endures.

455
00:25:54,724 --> 00:25:58,427
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes, for a day or two, even, the same mood will endure on the general level.

456
00:25:58,907 --> 00:26:01,209
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, next day, you notice, like, you don't feel right.

457
00:26:01,509 --> 00:26:03,590
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, there is discomfort fundamentally.

458
00:26:03,650 --> 00:26:04,691
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything is fine, actually.

459
00:26:05,411 --> 00:26:06,972
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing particularly bothering you.

460
00:26:07,453 --> 00:26:07,693
[SPEAKER_01]: But...

461
00:26:08,535 --> 00:26:15,239
[SPEAKER_01]: There is some weird, I don't know, discomfort or some weird anxiety or something at the back of your mind without anything specific.

462
00:26:16,079 --> 00:26:17,120
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's it.

463
00:26:17,360 --> 00:26:20,622
[SPEAKER_01]: Now you just recognize that change has occurred.

464
00:26:20,702 --> 00:26:20,942
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

465
00:26:21,402 --> 00:26:23,003
[SPEAKER_01]: As in now you're subjected to something else.

466
00:26:23,263 --> 00:26:23,444
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

467
00:26:24,304 --> 00:26:33,089
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you stop assuming outside of it, stop trying to get rid of it, stop trying to distract yourself from it, you become familiar with that very abrupt nature of change within which you are.

468
00:26:33,569 --> 00:26:34,870
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot conceive external.

469
00:26:35,931 --> 00:26:38,032
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you will develop this passion towards it.

470
00:26:41,519 --> 00:26:45,060
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there will be no self-centered point of view to maintain that passion anymore.

471
00:26:47,680 --> 00:26:50,901
[SPEAKER_01]: Because any point of view you have is within the present enduring feeling.

472
00:26:52,201 --> 00:26:59,723
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, any point of view you have is within the present enduring body that is the dark misery, pain, subject to change.

473
00:27:02,123 --> 00:27:03,024
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the same principle.

474
00:27:03,084 --> 00:27:08,505
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether you take it on the level of the feeling or on the level of the body, you want to see that you are within it, not the other way around.

475
00:27:09,497 --> 00:27:10,778
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, you are enclosed by it.

476
00:27:10,818 --> 00:27:18,563
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whether you look beyond it, your look and your thoughts of beyond it, is within the necessary basis of present enduring body.

477
00:27:19,623 --> 00:27:21,965
[SPEAKER_01]: It's within the necessary basis of present enduring feeling.

478
00:27:23,136 --> 00:27:30,078
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, the fact that present enduring feeling is felt differently at different times, means you have no say in its internal working, so to speak.

479
00:27:30,518 --> 00:27:31,238
[SPEAKER_01]: It's inconceivable.

480
00:27:31,958 --> 00:27:33,498
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can only be subjected to it.

481
00:27:33,518 --> 00:27:40,160
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you realize all you need to do is then restrain your effort, your attitude towards the feeling that's felt pleasantly.

482
00:27:40,180 --> 00:27:44,541
[SPEAKER_01]: Restrain... understand your attitude towards feeling that's felt unpleasantly.

483
00:27:44,561 --> 00:27:46,541
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, keep your attitude in check.

484
00:27:46,581 --> 00:27:48,962
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't distract yourself towards the feeling that's felt neutrally.

485
00:27:50,302 --> 00:27:53,804
[SPEAKER_01]: Because how these feelings are felt or why, it's actually irrelevant.

486
00:27:54,144 --> 00:28:00,668
[SPEAKER_01]: The only... what is relevant is that you are oppressed by them, which is why you automatically want to act and get rid of them, or prolong them and so on.

487
00:28:05,651 --> 00:28:09,433
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, he goes on to say, seeing in this way,

488
00:28:10,362 --> 00:28:17,103
[SPEAKER_00]: A noble disciple turns away from pleasant feeling, turns away from painful feeling, turns away from neither painful nor pleasant feeling.

489
00:28:17,683 --> 00:28:19,304
[SPEAKER_00]: Turning away, he becomes dispassionate.

490
00:28:19,324 --> 00:28:23,104
[SPEAKER_00]: Dispassionate, he's liberated.

491
00:28:23,704 --> 00:28:30,766
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, liberated, not oppressed anymore by any change that might occur within the five aggregates.

492
00:28:31,826 --> 00:28:37,147
[SPEAKER_00]: And then he goes, and he finishes, he says, in this way, Aggivesana, a bhikkhu with a liberated mind

493
00:28:37,754 --> 00:28:41,357
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't agree with anyone, doesn't disagree with anyone.

494
00:28:41,378 --> 00:28:45,181
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever is said in the world is expressed by him without holding.

495
00:28:49,906 --> 00:28:50,927
[SPEAKER_00]: He's fully independent.

496
00:28:54,325 --> 00:29:02,009
[SPEAKER_01]: Agreeing or disagreeing with anyone means basically adopting or relating to their view externally, to that extent.

497
00:29:02,609 --> 00:29:03,870
[SPEAKER_01]: But now you've abandoned everything.

498
00:29:03,890 --> 00:29:05,651
[SPEAKER_01]: Literally, whatever you said, it's factual.

499
00:29:06,971 --> 00:29:10,693
[SPEAKER_01]: Because none of it is rooted in trying to get rid of the feelings or repossess the body and so on.

500
00:29:12,162 --> 00:29:13,723
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's no agreement, no disagreement.

501
00:29:13,823 --> 00:29:14,524
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just factual.

502
00:29:14,584 --> 00:29:16,626
[SPEAKER_01]: So, two arahants, they don't agree with each other.

503
00:29:16,846 --> 00:29:17,106
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

504
00:29:17,687 --> 00:29:20,309
[SPEAKER_01]: They're two independent individuals, basically.

505
00:29:20,329 --> 00:29:20,489
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

506
00:29:21,450 --> 00:29:23,712
[SPEAKER_01]: Externally, yeah, they're in perfect agreement.

507
00:29:23,752 --> 00:29:23,972
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

508
00:29:23,992 --> 00:29:25,694
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they're not making effort to agree.

509
00:29:25,714 --> 00:29:26,214
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

510
00:29:28,116 --> 00:29:31,879
[SPEAKER_00]: But they're not actually agreeing with the other person's... another person's view or something.

511
00:29:31,899 --> 00:29:32,400
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

512
00:29:33,124 --> 00:29:34,425
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why they can't argue either.

513
00:29:35,406 --> 00:29:37,028
[SPEAKER_01]: Can't argue with someone.

514
00:29:37,808 --> 00:29:42,072
[SPEAKER_01]: It's either they're wrong or they'll agree with you, if you're an arahant.

515
00:29:42,513 --> 00:29:43,834
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're not agreeing with you.

516
00:29:44,474 --> 00:29:52,422
[SPEAKER_01]: They're agreeing with the universal nature of things, which is what you fully understood, developed, which is why you did abandon your sense of self and the whole thing, which is what makes you an arahant.

517
00:29:54,775 --> 00:30:03,106
[SPEAKER_01]: But yes, if you hear that, like if an onārān hears that, he can sound extremely conceited, as he did when the Buddha first basically spoke to that man.

518
00:30:03,586 --> 00:30:04,828
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, oh, you look very bright.

519
00:30:05,088 --> 00:30:06,149
[SPEAKER_01]: Whose teachings you follow?

520
00:30:06,770 --> 00:30:07,952
[SPEAKER_01]: I follow no one's teaching.

521
00:30:08,132 --> 00:30:09,093
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm my own leader.

522
00:30:09,233 --> 00:30:11,536
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm the supreme leader of gods and humans.

523
00:30:11,596 --> 00:30:12,518
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody is my match.

524
00:30:13,506 --> 00:30:14,106
[SPEAKER_01]: Pure fact.

525
00:30:14,447 --> 00:30:15,267
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

526
00:30:15,287 --> 00:30:16,688
[SPEAKER_01]: Purely factual statement.

527
00:30:16,708 --> 00:30:19,510
[SPEAKER_01]: He has understood the universal truth of it all.

528
00:30:19,890 --> 00:30:21,151
[SPEAKER_01]: Freed himself as a result of it.

529
00:30:21,551 --> 00:30:23,732
[SPEAKER_01]: But this man is like, oh, wow, okay.

530
00:30:24,032 --> 00:30:25,613
[SPEAKER_01]: So, good for you, friend.

531
00:30:25,633 --> 00:30:26,254
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

532
00:30:26,334 --> 00:30:26,994
[SPEAKER_01]: And dismissed it.

533
00:30:27,034 --> 00:30:27,414
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever.

534
00:30:27,715 --> 00:30:28,555
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever, yeah.

535
00:30:28,575 --> 00:30:29,996
[SPEAKER_01]: But he, this guy, is full of himself.

536
00:30:30,016 --> 00:30:30,256
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

537
00:30:30,276 --> 00:30:31,037
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how it sounds.

538
00:30:31,257 --> 00:30:31,457
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

